	<item>
		<title><![CDATA[Robert, Frances and Barbara Burkett]]></title>
		<link>https://allsaintsconcord.org/robert-frances-and-barbara-burkett/</link>
		<pubDate>Mon, 21 Jan 2019 22:21:00 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[BrianSapp-Moore]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://allsaintsconcord.org/?p=10859</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4>Southern Magnolia (Magnolia grandiflora)</h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><em>Given in memory of Robert, Frances and Barbara Burkett by Mary Frances Burkett.</em></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Description:</strong>  The southern magnolia is deeply rooted into the fabric of southern living.  A dense and flowering evergreen tree, it is grown as a specimen tree and can also be used for screening purposes.  It is a long-lived tree that grows to 60-70 feet over approximately 50 years.  Some trees grow as tall as 90 feet under optimal conditions.  It is a pyramidal-shaped tree with widths of 30-40 feet.  Several cultivars have been grown that demonstrate smaller size, cold hardiness, and variations in leaves and flowers.  </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Leaves:  </strong>Large, glossy in varying shades of green on the top and dull tan to copper brown underneath.  Alternate, simple ovate to elliptical leaves range from 5-10 inches long.  There is a felt-like fuzz on the underside of the leaves. </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Flowers:&nbsp;&nbsp;</strong>Large creamy white flowers range from 6-12 inches in size and are very fragrant. Blooms in late spring with lesser flowering throughout the summer.&nbsp;</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Fruit:</strong>  Cone-shaped fruiting cluster 3-5 inches long mature in late summer to early fall.  They release red coated seeds.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Habitat:</strong> Enjoys full sun and grows in acidic, loamy, moist, sandy, well-drained, and clay soils.  It has moderate drought tolerance.  </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Wildlife value:&nbsp;&nbsp;</strong>The fruit attracts songbirds.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Uses:&nbsp;&nbsp;</strong>Magnolia grandiflora is a popular ornamental tree that is grown for its attractive, shiny green leaves and large fragrant flowers.&nbsp;&nbsp;Both the green and brown sides of the leaves are used in Christmas wreaths.&nbsp;&nbsp;Look for them in the Christmas decorations of Colonial Williamsburg.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>History:</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;Until early 2018, a Southern Magnolia planted by President Andrew Jackson grew near the South Portico of the White House.&nbsp;&nbsp;Legend has it that it was planted as a seedling taken from The Hermitage, Jackson’s home in TN.&nbsp;&nbsp;It was the oldest tree on the White House Grounds.&nbsp;&nbsp;President Reagan gifted a cutting to his Chief of Staff, Howard Baker, and Michelle Obama donated a seedling to the “people’s garden” of the U.S. Department of Agriculture.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong><u>Sources</u></strong></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>NC State Extension Gardener Plant Toolbox.&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://plants.ces.ncsu.edu/plants/all/ilex-opaca/">https://plants.ces.ncsu.edu/plants/all/ilex-opaca/</a></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><a href="https://www.arborday.org/trees/treeguide/TreeDetail.cfm?ItemID=2452">https://www.arborday.org/trees/treeguide/TreeDetail.cfm?ItemID=2452</a></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magnolia_grandiflora">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magnolia_grandiflora</a></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->]]></content:encoded>
