	<item>
		<title><![CDATA[Jane Thomas]]></title>
		<link>https://allsaintsconcord.org/jane-thomas/</link>
		<pubDate>Wed, 26 Sep 2018 20:59:24 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[BrianSapp-Moore]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://allsaintsconcord.org/?p=10385</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4>Magnolia grandiflora (Southern Magnolia)</h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><em>This tree is dedicated by Nancy Hunter in loving memory of her mother, Jane Alma Rathbun Thomas.&nbsp;&nbsp;</em></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Description:</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;The southern magnolia is deeply rooted into the fabric of southern living.&nbsp;&nbsp;A dense, flowering evergreen tree it is grown as a specimen tree and can also be used for screening purposes.&nbsp;&nbsp;It is a long-lived tree that grows to 60-70 feet over approximately 50 years.&nbsp;&nbsp;Some trees grow as tall as 90 feet under optimal conditions.&nbsp;&nbsp;A pyramidal shaped tree with widths of 30-40 feet.&nbsp;&nbsp;Several cultivars have been grown that demonstrate smaller size, cold hardiness, and variations in leaves and flowers.&nbsp;&nbsp;‘Little Gem’ grows 20 feet tall by 10 feet wide and has a more compact, narrow upright form and works well in smaller landscape spaces.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Leaves:&nbsp;&nbsp;</strong>Large, glossy in varying shades of green on the top and dull tan to copper brown underneath.&nbsp;&nbsp;Alternate, simple ovate to elliptical leaves range from 5-10 inches long.&nbsp;&nbsp;There is a felt like fuzz on the underside of the leaves.&nbsp;</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Flowers:&nbsp;&nbsp;</strong>Large creamy white flowers range from 6-12 inches in size and are very fragrant. Blooms in late spring with lesser flowering throughout the summer.&nbsp;</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Fruit:</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;Cone shaped fruiting cluster 3-5 inches long mature in late summer to early fall.&nbsp;&nbsp;They release red coated seeds&nbsp;</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Habitat:</strong>&nbsp;enjoys full sun and grows in acidic, loamy, moist, sandy, well-drained, and clay soils.&nbsp;&nbsp;It has moderate drought tolerance.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Wildlife value:&nbsp;&nbsp;</strong>The fruit attracts songbirds.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Uses:&nbsp;&nbsp;</strong>Magnolia grandiflora is a popular ornamental tree that is grown for its attractive, shiny green leaves and large fragrant flowers.&nbsp;&nbsp;Both the green and brown sides of the leaves are used in Christmas wreaths.&nbsp;&nbsp;Look for them in the Christmas decorations of Colonial Williamsburg.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>History:</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;Until early 2018, a Southern Magnolia planted by President Andrew Jackson grew near the South Portico of the White House.&nbsp;&nbsp;Legend has it that it was planted as a seedling taken from The Hermitage, Jackson’s home in TN.&nbsp;&nbsp;It was the oldest tree on the White House Grounds.&nbsp;&nbsp;President Reagan gifted a cutting to his Chief of Staff, Howard Baker, and Michelle Obama donated a seedling to the “peoples garden” of the U.S. Department of Agriculture.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Sources</strong> </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>NC State Extension Gardener Plant Toolbox.&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://plants.ces.ncsu.edu/plants/all/ilex-opaca/">https://plants.ces.ncsu.edu/plants/all/ilex-opaca/</a></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><a href="https://www.arborday.org/trees/treeguide/TreeDetail.cfm?ItemID=2452">https://www.arborday.org/trees/treeguide/TreeDetail.cfm?ItemID=2452</a></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magnolia_grandiflora">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magnolia_grandiflora</a></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->]]></content:encoded>
		<excerpt:encoded><![CDATA[]]></excerpt:encoded>
